* Dogfood task 2026-07-18: an ordinary hand-written 6809 assembly OS-9
* program, not tied to a specific skill claim (contrast with
* signal-reserved-range.a in this same directory). Opens a file for
* reading using raw I$ syscalls directly (I$Open/I$ReadLn/I$Close, no
* BASIC09 involved), counts total lines and total characters (excluding
* the CR line terminator each line ends with), and prints a two-line
* decimal summary via I$WritLn. Filename comes in as a command-line
* parameter, read directly from the entry-time X/D registers -- no
* PARAM statement, this is what PARAM compiles down to.
*
* Assembled with `asm` (Standard NitrOS-9 6809/6309 Assembler), the only
* one of the two available 6809 assemblers that works through this REPL
* -- `rma` hangs indefinitely on this setup, see
* 6809/assembly-and-tools.md. `asm lncnt.a -O=lncnt` (the leading `-`
* on `O=` means silent overwrite); the object module lands in CMDS
* (the execution directory), not the current/data directory.
*
* Two facts used here were NOT in the skill going in and had to be
* determined live (see dogfood-report-asm-2026-07-18.md for the full
* story):
*   1. Command-line parameter register convention (undocumented in the
*      skill for 6809): at entry, X points at a raw parameter area
*      that starts with a single leading SPACE (the separator after
*      the module name), then the literal argument text, then a
*      trailing CR; D's low byte (== B) is the total byte count of
*      that whole area (space + text + CR). This program peeks the
*      first byte and skips it only if it actually is a space, then
*      copies up to a CR/space/32-char cap into its own buffer and
*      sign-bit-terminates the last copied byte before handing it to
*      I$Open -- robust either way, and confirmed live via the OS-9
*      debugger's `E`/`M` commands (raw memory dump showed the bytes
*      20 6C 63 63 6F 75 6E 74 31 0D = " lccount1\r" starting at X).
*   2. I$ReadLn's returned Y (actual line length) INCLUDES the
*      terminating CR, unlike BASIC09's own READ statement (which
*      strips it) -- found by comparing this program's raw char count
*      against an independent fsize-based count, off by exactly one
*      per line. Fixed by subtracting 1 from Y before accumulating,
*      guarded against underflow on a theoretical zero return.
*
* One real bug found and fixed via the debugger, not guessing: the
* first version of the decimal-print routine (PRDEC) reloaded the
* 16-bit value being divided via `LDD VALUE,u` on every inner-loop
* pass, not realizing LDD clobbers BOTH halves of D -- including A,
* which was also being used (via INCA) as the digit accumulator for
* that same inner loop. Every iteration silently wiped out the digit
* count and replaced it with whatever the high byte of the
* (partially-subtracted, wrapping) value happened to be, producing
* garbage punctuation characters (",///" etc.) instead of digits. This
* only showed up as data corruption, not a crash or assembler error --
* found by single-stepping via debugger breakpoints (`B <addr>` / `G`)
* at rdend/prdec/prdin/prddn and inspecting live register/memory state
* with `:` and `M`, not by re-reading the source. Fixed by moving the
* digit accumulator to its own scratch byte (DIGIT,u) instead of
* sharing a register with the value being divided.
*
* An earlier, unrelated bug (fixed first, before this one): three
* short `BEQ noparm` branches were out-of-range once the real read/
* print logic pushed `noparm` more than 127 bytes away -- `asm` caught
* this at assemble time ("***** Error: out of range"), fixed by
* switching those three to `LBEQ`.
*
* An even earlier bug, in a throwaway probe program (pdump.a, not this
* file) written first to determine the parameter-register convention:
* forgetting the module header's declared total size must include the
* module's own trailing 3-byte CRC (MOD's first operand needs to be a
* label defined *after* `EMOD`, or `label+3` for one defined before)
* produced a module with a corrupted/misaligned header -- `ident`
* showed a blank name field, bad CRC, and garbage Edition byte. A
* second bug in that same probe (a loop counter sharing register B
* with a subroutine that also clobbered B, with no save/restore)
* caused an actual unbounded write loop that overflowed the module's
* small scratch data area and hung the emulated CPU solid, requiring
* `nitros9repl.sh restart` to recover.
*
* Test input file (created via `tee`, content typed directly, verified
* independently with `list` + `fsize` before ever running this
* program against it):
*
*   lccount1 (3 lines):
*     Hello from 6809 assembly     (24 chars)
*     Second line of test data     (24 chars)
*     Line three of the test file  (27 chars)
*   fsize lccount1 -> 78 Bytes in 1 Files  (75 chars + 3 CR = 78, matches)
*
*   lccount2 (5 lines, including one deliberately blank trailing line):
*     One
*     Two two
*     Three three three
*     Four four four four
*     <blank>
*   fsize lccount2 -> 51 Bytes in 1 Files  (46 chars + 5 CR = 51, matches)
*
* Literal program output, both live on real NitrOS-9 (6809) via
* nitros9repl.sh + asm:
*
*   lncnt lccount1  ->
*     Lines: 00003
*     Chars: 00075
*
*   lncnt lccount2  ->
*     Lines: 00005
*     Chars: 00046
*
* Both exactly match the independent fsize/list-based counts above.
* Error paths also verified live:
*   lncnt              -> Error: no filename given
*   lncnt doesnotexist123 -> Error: cannot open file

 nam LnCnt
 mod eom,nm,$11,$81,start,$0100
nm fcs /LnCnt/
PARMPTR equ 0
MAXLEN equ 2
FNLEN equ 3
FNBUF equ 4
LINEBUF equ 40
LINECNT equ 140
CHARCNT equ 142
TMPY equ 144
PATHNUM equ 146
VALUE equ 148
DIGIT equ 150
OUTBUF equ 151
start stx PARMPTR,u
 tstb
 lbeq noparm
 subb #1
 lbeq noparm
 cmpb #32
 ble lenok
 ldb #32
lenok stb MAXLEN,u
 clr FNLEN,u
 leax FNBUF,u
 ldy PARMPTR,u
 lda ,y
 cmpa #$20
 bne skipsp
 leay 1,y
skipsp equ *
cpylp lda ,y+
 cmpa #$20
 beq cpydn
 cmpa #$0D
 beq cpydn
 sta ,x+
 inc FNLEN,u
 ldb FNLEN,u
 cmpb MAXLEN,u
 bge cpydn
 bra cpylp
cpydn lda FNLEN,u
 lbeq noparm
 leax FNBUF,u
 ldb FNLEN,u
 decb
 abx
 lda ,x
 ora #$80
 sta ,x
 leax FNBUF,u
 lda #1
 swi2
 fcb $84
 bcc openok
 leax operr,pcr
 ldy #operrl
 lda #1
 swi2
 fcb $8C
 clrb
 swi2
 fcb $06
openok sta PATHNUM,u
 clr LINECNT,u
 clr LINECNT+1,u
 clr CHARCNT,u
 clr CHARCNT+1,u
rdloop lda PATHNUM,u
 leax LINEBUF,u
 ldy #90
 swi2
 fcb $8B
 bcs rdend
 ldd LINECNT,u
 addd #1
 std LINECNT,u
 cmpy #0
 beq skipdec
 leay -1,y
skipdec sty TMPY,u
 ldd CHARCNT,u
 addd TMPY,u
 std CHARCNT,u
 bra rdloop
rdend lda PATHNUM,u
 swi2
 fcb $8F
 leax OUTBUF,u
 leay lmsg,pcr
 ldb #lmsgl
 bsr copys
 ldd LINECNT,u
 std VALUE,u
 bsr prdec
 lda #$0D
 sta ,x+
 leax OUTBUF,u
 ldy #40
 lda #1
 swi2
 fcb $8C
 leax OUTBUF,u
 leay cmsg,pcr
 ldb #cmsgl
 bsr copys
 ldd CHARCNT,u
 std VALUE,u
 bsr prdec
 lda #$0D
 sta ,x+
 leax OUTBUF,u
 ldy #40
 lda #1
 swi2
 fcb $8C
 clrb
 swi2
 fcb $06
noparm leax noparmm,pcr
 ldy #noparml
 lda #1
 swi2
 fcb $8C
 clrb
 swi2
 fcb $06
copys lda ,y+
 sta ,x+
 decb
 bne copys
 rts
prdec leay dectbl,pcr
 ldb #5
prdout pshs b
 clr DIGIT,u
prdin ldd VALUE,u
 subd ,y
 bcs prddn
 std VALUE,u
 inc DIGIT,u
 bra prdin
prddn lda DIGIT,u
 adda #$30
 sta ,x+
 leay 2,y
 puls b
 decb
 bne prdout
 rts
dectbl fdb 10000,1000,100,10,1
lmsg fcc /Lines: /
lmsgl equ *-lmsg
cmsg fcc /Chars: /
cmsgl equ *-cmsg
operr fcc /Error: cannot open file/
 fcb $0D
operrl equ *-operr
noparmm fcc /Error: no filename given/
 fcb $0D
noparml equ *-noparmm
 emod
eom equ *
